WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.920
Test-Time Compute for Humanoid Behavior Foundation Models.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.391
Even when the command matches the reference, execution can differ.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.247
Can optimizing the command close this gap?

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.749
We sample commands and evaluate their rollouts in parallel.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.959
Lower-cost candidates guide the next proposal.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.199
After I iterations, we execute E steps of the best command found, then replan.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:41.550
Optimizing the commands brings execution closer to the reference, in simulation and on the real robot.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.577
The same improvement appears in backward walking, where optimized commands better recover the intended displacement.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.133
Better tracking, without further training.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.435
More compute can improve tracking. How should we allocate it?

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.807
Together, N, I, H, and E define a configuration and its compute budget.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.929
Our strategy stays close to the empirical optimum, with four percent mean regret.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.649
With more compute, measured cost falls by fifty-eight percent.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.142
Used unchanged, the strategy generalizes to new motions and BFMs.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.585
A high-level policy learns commands from demonstrations.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.432
But the command-execution gap means recorded motion cannot directly serve as command labels.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.737
Original command labels train a high-level policy that runs with SONIC.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:54.469
We optimize commands for SONIC using recorded motion as the reference. These commands become the training labels.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:03.607
With SONIC, optimized labels approach original-command performance and outperform robot-state labels.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.545
Our method also enables demonstration reuse across BFMs.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.181
Using the same SONIC demonstrations, we re-optimize commands for HEFT.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.621
We evaluate the learned policies on four real-world loco-manipulation tasks.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.361
Execution uses SONIC in the top row and HEFT in the bottom row.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.767
The first task is to place a cup in the basket.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.476
Next, place the parcel bag on the shelf.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.741
Now, place the basket on the shelf.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.784
Finally, put the bottle in the drawer.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:54.215
To conclude, we improve tracking, make better use of test-time compute, and reuse demonstrations to train high-level policies.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:56.816
Thanks for watching.
